Each run used the gate bound and restored the product files byte for byte. The two front matter runs used the script of `feat(markdown): keep a Markdown file's YAML or TOML front matter`. Each earlier run also ran before the lockfile moved to mdast-util-to-markdown 2.2.3, so its round-trip counts are those of the 2.1.2 writer. The 7bc3175013 run used the script of `fix(markdown): round-trip every CommonMark and GFM spec example`, the unclosed tag run the script of `fix(markdown): keep a streamed tag open until its real closer`, and the `remarkSeparateEscapes` run the script of `fix(markdown): read an escaped URL as text`. The `openList` and 0cfbdc701a runs used the script of `fix(markdown): read CR line endings as LF and keep a bare URL a link`, and the others ran at the first sync, `test(platejs): check Markdown round trips and streamed parses on every small document`.

Mutations planted in `runParsedNodes` of `markdownConversion.ts`, on the partial parse only, an earlier repair and two changes to the check's own files showed where the three preview rules hold:

- Dropping an escaped star with its backslash from the source that a partial parse reads gives 14 `markdown-stream-lossless` findings. On `**a \* b` the preview of the first 6 characters shows `**a` where the strict parse shows `**a *`.
- Dropping every star inside a code span gives 3, all on ``**a `b*c` d``.
- Adding a newline after the last text of every preview gives 24,520, and so does adding the stand-in character there, which prints `shows 1 stand-in characters (U+E000), not 0`.
- Adding a newline to the preview of `<callout>\n![a](b)` alone gives 1. The default strict parse refuses that prefix, so the rule reads it only because it parses under the preview's own loss policy.
- Hiding a backslash that another backslash escapes gives 1, on `a\\**b**`. Hiding one unescaped backslash that ends the prefix gives none.
- Hiding every star outside code gives 34 on six sources, each with an escaped star or a star in a code block, and none where the source wrote the star unescaped.
- Hiding the two stars of the prefix `a**`, one character after the preview of `a*` showed the first, gives one more `markdown-stream-prefix` finding on `a**b**`, a source the baseline lists, and no lossless finding.
- The first repair of half-typed bold, which never landed, gives 80 `markdown-stream-lossless` findings. On `- [ ] a\n**a**` its preview of the first 10 characters shows `a` where the strict parse shows `a\n**`, so it hides a line ending with the stars.
- A script whose `completesDefinition` never matches excuses nothing, finds 2,683 `markdown-stream-prefix` entries and exits 2. The gate on a baseline without the three rules' entries prints all 2,467 take-backs and 72 hidden runs with their details.

Each of these runs used the gate bound, in a scratch worktree at `fix(platejs): keep a continued Markdown preview equal to a fresh one` with the script that added the three rules, and restored the product files byte for byte.

Two classifiers planted in `packages/platejs/scripts/markdown-round-trip-previews.ts`, and the codec of a repair that never landed, showed where the guess rule holds:

- A classifier that calls a take-back a guess whenever the earlier preview is the later text with only markers left out fails four cases of `markdown-round-trip-previews.spec.ts`. They are a star the next preview invents in the middle, a star more than the strict parse of the prefix shows, another marker than the one the prefix holds, and a styled preview that the next one shows raw though the final parse styles it.
- A classifier that compares the texts without their whitespace fails one case, a preview that drops a line ending with the markers.
- The second repair of half-typed styling kept a classifier that read only the first character that differs. With that repair's codec, four take-backs that its classifier filed as guesses stay `markdown-stream-prefix` findings. `[^1]: n\n\n**a [^1] b**` shows `n\n**a` and the footnote's object after `n\na [^1` at 17 characters. `[a]: u\n\n*b **[a]** c*` shows `*b **a` after `b [a` at 16. `**[\*x](u)**` shows `*x` after `[*x](u` at 10. The fourth is planted: after a preview of `**hello world` that shows `hello world`, the final text is `*`.

Each of these runs used a scratch worktree at `test(platejs): check what each streamed Markdown preview shows` with the script that added the guess rule.

